docs/fse-pod2-results.md:91:Python peak RSS is the encoder interpreter process high-water mark. Rust peak RSS uses the Python parent's `RUSAGE_CHILDREN`, labelled native Rust process; it excludes that Python parent's own memory. Do not treat these as equal-scope total application memory, sum them as simultaneous peaks, or infer that the Rust controller consumes no memory. Artifact bytes are the finished fse artifact versus the counterpart's safetensors weights, not complete installed library or deployment footprints. Full lifecycle and consistent process-tree memory comparisons remain open.
docs/benchmarks/dr151-pod4-plan.md:1:# Pod 4 fix-up plan, not launched
docs/benchmarks/dr151-pod4-plan.md:15:Retain full output checks with bounded-memory blocks. Large matrices must not be promoted wholesale to float64 during validation. Output checking and serialization are outside encoding timing. Record actual per-call durations, not a normalized duration that hides many short calls.
docs/research/runtime-prior-art.md:13:- **Batching:** fixed slices of `batch_size=1024` sentences ([L303-L306](https://github.com/MinishLab/model2vec/blob/dc1ff382f279/model2vec/model.py#L303-L306), [`_batch` L487](https://github.com/MinishLab/model2vec/blob/dc1ff382f279/model2vec/model.py#L487)). No memory-based bound.
docs/research/runtime-prior-art.md:17:- **Streaming and back-pressure:** none. The input must be a `Sequence`, and all outputs stay in memory until the final concatenation. joblib's default `pre_dispatch="2 * n_jobs"` limits how many tasks are dispatched at once ([parallel.py L1048](https://github.com/joblib/joblib/blob/878c9e05995b/joblib/parallel.py#L1048), [L1227](https://github.com/joblib/joblib/blob/878c9e05995b/joblib/parallel.py#L1227)). model2vec still materializes the full list of batch slices first (`list(self._batch(...))`).
docs/research/runtime-prior-art.md:25:- **Multi-process:** `encode_multi_process` is deprecated. `encode(pool=...)` or `encode(device=[...])` is used instead ([model.py L887-L891](https://github.com/huggingface/sentence-transformers/blob/145ef47d5a4b/sentence_transformers/sentence_transformer/model.py#L887-L891), [L1094-L1098](https://github.com/huggingface/sentence-transformers/blob/145ef47d5a4b/sentence_transformers/sentence_transformer/model.py#L1094-L1098)). The pool consists of `spawn` processes, one per target device, fed by one shared input queue and one output queue. The model is moved to CPU and put in shared memory first ([base/model.py L1833-L1862](https://github.com/huggingface/sentence-transformers/blob/145ef47d5a4b/sentence_transformers/base/model.py#L1833-L1862)).
docs/research/runtime-prior-art.md:128:6. **A memory-sized task bound (about 4 MiB of output)** where everyone else counts sentences, rows or words. Only tokenizers 1.0 sizes tasks in bytes (input bytes) and Arrow applies back-pressure in bytes. Sources: [tokenizers](https://github.com/huggingface/tokenizers/blob/d9a5e8af4b58/tokenizers/tk-encode/src/tokenizer/pipeline/parallel.rs#L302-L421), [Arrow](https://github.com/apache/arrow/blob/fc390cd3442b/cpp/src/arrow/acero/options.h#L353-L356).
docs/fse-comparison.md:16:The comparison includes model2vec/POTION, model2vec-rs, FastEmbed, Sentence Transformers StaticEmbedding and all-MiniLM-L6-v2, TF-IDF, a fastText filter classifier, FAISS and another local search library. It includes one-worker and all-core CPU encoding, startup, memory, long texts, whole-lifecycle latency, numerical subspaces and recall, and estimated compute cost.
docs/fse-comparison.md:67:its durable store during construction. FAISS and hnswlib construct in memory
docs/fse-comparison.md:170:The current local quality plan is `benchmarks/baseline/dr151_quality_priority_plan_20261101.json`. It supersedes the initial 35-configuration plan. The final scope contains eleven full float32 model configurations and twenty reduced quantized configurations. Eight full float32 configurations have completed the local 53-task scope. Reduced float32 references are additional calibrations for matched quantization deltas. They do not add model/dtype combinations. Local full runs exclude MIND, which remains a separate unmodified-loader pod task.
docs/fse-comparison.md:212:MIND is **not computed: MTEB 2.26.25 MindSmallReranking needs more memory than the 64 GB pod (two attempts, status 137 and earlier ENOSPC)**. No further MIND run is planned. English means cover 40 of 41 tasks, with no substituted score. Historical deferred records remain unchanged. See the [Pod 3 and MIND evidence](fse-pod3-results.md) for committed logs and the surviving provenance.
docs/research/static-embeddings-2018-2026.md:59:- **Quality (paper, their MTEB English benchmark, Avg-s2s / Avg).** Ours-256 63.76 / 51.97; Ours-512 64.06 / 52.35; Model2Vec (potion-base-32M) 62.37 / 51.33; WordLlama 60.40 / 50.23; SimCSE 62.75 / 48.86; MiniLM-L6 65.95 / 56.26. Ablation: word-level PCA 48.1 versus sentence-level PCA 49.9 on MTEB Avg. They do not compare against SIF, uSIF or GloVe.
docs/research/mlx-metal-kernels.md:90:- **Gather.** Layout: row-major `(V, D)` word table, one thread per (sentence, dim) with consecutive `d` in consecutive threads (coalesced 128 B segments per token row); `tokens` and `offsets` read per thread (cached; all threads of a sentence read the same ones). 391k tokens in 9.3 ms (42 M tokens/s) without tuning. Variants to try in the PR: a SIMD-group per sentence with `float4` loads (D=300 is a multiple of 4; D=100 is too; must keep per-element chains), shared `tokens` via threadgroup memory, 2D grid ordering so that the table row stays in cache; none was measured by me.
docs/research/mlx-metal-kernels.md:91:- **Unified memory.** `mx.array(np_array)` copies (29 ms for 64 MB). `mx.asarray(x)` (default `copy=None`) adopts the numpy buffer without copy when it is **page-aligned** (16,384 bytes on this machine) and otherwise copies; `copy=False` raises if it cannot adopt (`ValueError`), and refuses dtype conversions (MLX 0.32.1+, `python/tests/test_zero_copy.py`). A mutation of the source is visible in the array afterwards (measured), so the Python side must keep the buffer alive and unchanged until the evaluation is done; for reading results, `np.array(out)` copies; a zero-copy view of the result is not something I verified. For the vocabulary table (uploaded once in `prepare`) a copy is fine (240 MB in 28 to 42 ms). Output buffers allocated by MLX each call (and kept in MLX's cache); to avoid per-task allocation churn, MLX's allocator caches freed buffers (see 6).
docs/research/mlx-metal-kernels.md:97:- **MLX specifics.** Lazy graph; `mx.eval(outs)` encodes and commits; it blocks until done. `mx.async_eval` exists (starts and returns; check the docs of that call for the memory semantics before using it); `mx.synchronize(stream)` waits. Thread model (MLX 0.32.4): each thread has its own default stream; `new_stream` is bound to the creating thread, `new_thread_unsafe_stream` is usable anywhere but "all nodes in a graph must be evaluated in sequence and it is user's responsibility to ensure there is no race condition", `new_thread_local_stream` makes one per thread (docstrings in `python/src/stream.cpp`). Thread safety was declared in 0.31.2 (issue 2133 closed 2026-04-24: "MLX has thread safety support in 0.31.2 now"; the closing comment does not list what is covered). Before that there were crashes with concurrent `mx.eval` (issues 2067, 3078, 3216). Issue 3727: from 0.31.2 a stream created in the main thread fails in a worker thread (closed as an mlx-lm fix; the cause is the stream thread affinity described above, which still holds in my 0.32.4 test).
docs/research/mlx-metal-kernels.md:106:- **Errors inside kernels:** no bounds checking (an out-of-range `a[t+100000000u]` read returned 0 without error in my test); a failed Metal command buffer (GPU timeout or memory pressure, watchdog) calls `check_error` from a completion handler, which throws on a libdispatch thread and **aborts the process** (MLX issue 3390, April 2026; maintainer: needs exception-safe `CommandEncoder`; workaround env `AGX_RELAX_CDM_CTXSTORE_TIMEOUT=1`; merged into issue 2670, status afterwards unverified). The runtime cannot recover from it, so bound the kernel duration per task (tasks of at most 16 MiB of rows already bound it) and validate all indices on the host.
docs/research/mlx-metal-kernels.md:107:- **Memory:** MLX caches freed buffers; `mx.get_cache_memory()`, `mx.set_cache_limit(bytes)`, `mx.clear_cache()`, `mx.set_memory_limit(bytes)` (default 1.5 x the recommended working set; allocations beyond raise), `mx.set_wired_limit` (macOS 15+) (`python/src/memory.cpp`). The old `mx.metal.*` names are deprecated aliases. The cache limit defaults to the memory limit, so a long run with changing buffer sizes can keep much memory; a backend should set a modest cache limit around its own run or call `clear_cache()` when a pipeline closes, and count the device memory of the window. The vocabulary table's device copy is an MLX array owned by the prepared object: release on close.
docs/research/mlx-metal-kernels.md:128:- MLX source (clone at 7d2d94a, 2026-10-28): https://github.com/ml-explore/mlx, `python/src/fast.cpp` (metal_kernel and its docstring), `mlx/backend/common/metal_kernel.cpp`, `mlx/backend/metal/custom_kernel.cpp`, `mlx/backend/metal/device.cpp` (set_compile_options, get_library, get_metal_version, load_device), `mlx/backend/metal/kernels/CMakeLists.txt` (`-fno-fast-math`), `mlx/stream.h`, `python/src/stream.cpp`, `python/src/memory.cpp`, `python/src/ops.cpp` (asarray), `python/tests/test_zero_copy.py`, `python/tests/test_fast.py`, `docs/src/usage/{precision,unified_memory,using_streams,environment_variables}.rst`, `.github/workflows/release.yml`, `CMakeLists.txt`, `setup.py`
docs/research/vector-db-usearch-hnswlib.md:78:- Intel Sapphire Rapids, `nk_dot_i8` (`dot/README.md:222-227`): serial 16.9 / 16.8 / 15.6; haswell 43.2 / 35.8 / 29.1; skylake 52.9 / 36.5 / 28.5; icelake (VNNI) 64.0 / 46.2 / 26.8; alder 42.8 / 40.4 / 31.1 gb/s. At 4096 elements all of them fall to cache/memory limits and VNNI is no better than Skylake-X.
docs/research/vector-db-usearch-hnswlib.md:81:- USearch README exact-search claim (`README.md:188-192`): "up to 20x" vs `faiss.IndexFlatL2` in Google Colab, 55.3 ms vs 2.54 ms, linked to GitHub issue 176. I read the thread: the 20x is a user's own report of 500 vectors x 1024 d (`l2sq`, "index has to be recreated every time"), so FAISS's number includes building the index per call; USearch's maintainer answered on an M2 MacBook. **Do not cite this as a brute-force kernel speedup.** [read, conclusion mine]
docs/research/vector-db-usearch-hnswlib.md:112:- AVX IP (`space_ip.h:24-69`): 2 loads x 2 per iteration but **one accumulator** `sum256 = add(sum256, mul(v1, v2))` (no FMA), a dependency chain of 2 adds per 16 floats, then a 128-bit tail and a horizontal add through memory. AVX-512 IP (`:144-200`): unrolled 4x with `_mm512_fmadd_ps` into **one** accumulator, `_mm512_reduce_add_ps`. AVX-512 L2 (`space_l2.h:24-60`) deliberately uses `add(mul)` with the FMA line commented out (`:51`). Single-accumulator chains are latency-bound; NumKong's tile design (4 independent accumulators) is the fix, and fse's `dot16` (16 lanes) is already a wide-accumulator design. [read]
docs/research/vector-db-usearch-hnswlib.md:124:- Level 0 stores, per element, one contiguous record `[link count + maxM0 neighbour ids][vector][label]` (`size_links_level0_ = maxM0_*sizeof(tableint)+sizeof(linklistsizeint)`, `size_data_per_element_ = size_links_level0_ + data_size_ + sizeof(labeltype)`, `:126-129`). Reading a node's links and its vector touches adjacent memory, and a candidate's links and data come from the same place. [read]
docs/research/profiling-tools.md:1:# Profiling setup research (CPU + memory, Python + native frames, per thread)
docs/research/profiling-tools.md:32:- Hooked allocators (source): malloc, calloc, realloc, free, valloc, posix_memalign, aligned_alloc, mmap, munmap; mmap64/memalign/pvalloc only on Linux/glibc. mremap not seen in the part read. https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bloomberg/memray/main/src/memray/_memray/hooks.cpp . So malloc from Cython is seen; mmap calls are seen as allocations of the mapped size, but file-backed pages are not RSS-accurate: docs say a profiler cannot see when the OS makes pages resident ("resident memory will only increase once you actually write to that memory"), https://bloomberg.github.io/memray/memory.html. Whether memray flags file-backed vs anonymous mmap: UNVERIFIED. Memray records RSS snapshots over time (changelog mentions VmRSS snapshots; https://bloomberg.github.io/memray/changelog.html, snippet only).
docs/research/profiling-tools.md:35:- Scalene memory: own allocator interposition, splits Python vs native, leak detector experimental. https://github.com/plasma-umass/scalene
docs/research/profiling-tools.md:36:- Peak RSS: `resource.getrusage(RUSAGE_CHILDREN).ru_maxrss` counts only terminated and waited-for children (https://docs.python.org/3/library/resource.html) and is in bytes on macOS but kilobytes on Linux (https://www.python.org/pipermail/python-bugs-list/2017-May/336429.html). It returns the max over children, not a sum (UNVERIFIED here). `/usr/bin/time -l` (macOS, bytes) and `-v` (GNU, KB) wrap the same counter (UNVERIFIED). For RSS over time, poll psutil `Process.memory_info().rss` plus children every 50 to 100 ms in a sidecar (psutil not verified this session); for mmap-heavy code also record USS/PSS where available.
docs/research/profiling-tools.md:51:- Implications: any local process of the same user could then read the memory of that Python, so do it only on a dedicated profiling interpreter copy, never on the shared uv Python; signature changes break on uv reinstall.
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:7:Method and honesty notes. I read docs/architecture/store.md, "sentence_vectors 1", `_store/_search.py`, `_shard.py` and `_native_kernel_search.h`, then fetched primary docs/source (list at the end). Statements tagged **[verified]** come from a page I fetched this session. **[memory]** is my background knowledge, not re-checked. **[derived]** is my own reasoning or arithmetic, not a published result. **[unverified]** is a claim from a secondary source or a search snippet. No benchmark was run. Where the fetch tool summarised a page, numbers are as reported by that summary and worth a second look before they go into a PR description.
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:27:- Also in FAISS: `exhaustive_L2sqr_fused_cmax` fuses distance and 1-NN selection to avoid storing temporary dot products; only for small d (PQ training). [verified]
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:44:- Hardware: M1 supports dot-product (SDOT); I8MM (SMMLA) I believe arrives with M2/ARMv8.6 [memory, check `sysctl hw.optional.arm.FEAT_I8MM` before designing]. BLAS libraries do not offer int8 GEMM on Accelerate [memory], so SMMLA/VNNI matrix tiles would be hand-written.
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:51:- Exact: BOND yes. fse relation: poor fit. A new row layout would break `sentence_vectors 1` and the memory-mapped row-major scan; cosine on unit rows needs a different bound; our float order contract forbids reordering dimensions. Rank low. A cheaper cousin is 3.1.
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:55:- fse already blocks rows x queries to about 4 MiB of scores and reads each row once per query block. FAISS blocks queries by 4096 and database by 1024 in the BLAS path (above). Nothing to adopt beyond tuning: [derived] with 10 workers and 258 MB of int8 data at 859,346 x 300, one query costs 10-13 ms, i.e. about 20-25 GB/s of row traffic. M1 Pro's memory bandwidth is advertised at 200 GB/s [memory], so a bandwidth-bound int8 scan would be several times faster; the current kernel is compute-bound by the decode (`int8 -> float`) plus the 16-lane fma chain. That gap is the opportunity of 3.1.
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:68:- Expected effect: [derived, not measured] the scan becomes memory-bandwidth bound instead of decode/fma bound; plausibly 2 to 5x at 1 to 8 queries on int8 stores. Float32/float16 stores cannot use it (no integer rows); a float16 variant would need an fp16 dot (NEON has FMLA.8H but accumulating in fp16 loses precision, which NumKong avoids for general dot products [verified]).
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:74:- What: keep 1 bit per dimension (sign) in a sidecar, scan with XOR+popcount, take `oversample * k` candidates, rescore with stored vectors. Qdrant docs: binary quantization 32x memory reduction, "up to a 40x speedup" (their claim), recall@100 0.98 at 4x oversampling for ada-002 1536d, 0.9966 at 3x for text-embedding-3-large 3072d; for 512-d text-embedding-3-small only 53 to 55% without and 71 to 91% with rescoring; they recommend oversampling 3 to start. Higher-dimension models do far better. [verified, qdrant.tech quantization guide]
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:95:- Qdrant: vectors are always in a memory-mapped file; `cached` tier preloads them into the page cache at startup, `cold` does not; `memmap_threshold` chooses the default. [verified, storage docs]
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:97:- A community gist for Qdrant found `MADV_WILLNEED` showed no visible improvement on the first search, whereas `MAP_POPULATE` pre-populated memory (cold cache cost about 45 s extra for 20.9 GB, 10M x 512d). [unverified, community gist]
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:98:- fse relation: the first search is disk-bound. A flat scan is perfectly sequential per shard, the case where `MADV_SEQUENTIAL`/readahead helps and `MADV_RANDOM` hurts. Options: (a) `VectorStore.warm()` reading the files with a few threads ahead of the search (works on every OS, can be overlapped with the first query by a prefetch thread that stays N blocks ahead of the workers), (b) `mmap.madvise(MADV_SEQUENTIAL/WILLNEED)` where available (Python's `mmap` has `.madvise`; numpy memmap exposes `_mmap`; macOS honors fewer hints; `F_RDADVISE`/`F_RDAHEAD` via `fcntl` [memory]), (c) `MAP_POPULATE`/`MADV_POPULATE_READ` on Linux [memory].
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:109:- Row alignment/padding to 64 B (SIMD loads and cache lines): a 300-byte int8 row straddles cache lines. Padding to 320 costs 6.7% more bytes and would change `sentence_vectors 1` (shape/dtype contract) so only a 2.x option. Benefit unmeasured; common in FAISS/ScaNN code paths that pad dims to multiples of the SIMD width [memory, unverified]. Skip until 3.1 exists and shows misaligned loads in the profile.
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:121:1. Segments plus WAL (Qdrant): writes go first to a WAL with sequence numbers, then to segments; appendable vs non-appendable (immutable) segments; per-point versions let it replay after an unclean shutdown; an optimizer converts and merges segments. [verified, storage concepts page] Lance: fragments with manifests and ACID commits. [verified overview; compaction/atomic-rename details not in the fetched page, **[memory]** that Lance exposes a compaction call]. fse has no WAL by design; a crash leaves a valid prefix of whole shards, which is the stronger simplicity.
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:123:3. Durability vs throughput switch (`durable=False`): the standard dial in databases (SQLite `synchronous`, `PRAGMA fullfsync` on macOS, PostgreSQL `synchronous_commit`, RocksDB `WriteOptions::sync`) [memory; docs not fetched this session]. For fse: skip `F_FULLFSYNC`/`fsync` of each shard and of the directory, keep the exclusive rename so readers still see whole-or-nothing at the *process* level; a power cut can then leave a published shard whose data never reached disk, so `verify` (xxh3-128 over the data) becomes the recovery tool. Cheaper variants that stay durable: (a) overlap the sync of shard N with the writing of shard N+1 (the publish thread already runs separately), (b) one directory sync per batch of shards instead of per shard. Impact: at 256 MiB shards the fsync is amortized over about 0.4 s of writing at 2.4 M rows/s with 300-d int8 rows [derived: 256 MiB / 300 B = 895k rows], so the gain is mostly for small shards or `seal`-heavy streams; measure `F_FULLFSYNC` cost on the target SSD before building. Effort low (`_writer.py`, `_shard.py`, `_container/_npy_inplace.py`, a `durable` argument on `ShardSink`, docs/architecture/store.md, "sentence_vectors 1"). The AGENTS.md rule "validate strictly" suggests refusing `durable=False` for `seal()`'s promise, or documenting that `durable_rows` then means "published", not "synced".
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:126:6. Preallocation/`posix_fallocate`/`F_PREALLOCATE` to avoid fragmentation and `ENOSPC` late; fse writes the header for the full shard and shrinks at the end (sparse file). [memory, no source consulted]. Low priority.
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:133:- Apple GPU (MLX/Metal) [derived, speculative]: the 256-query BLAS case is about 1.3e11 FLOP (256 x 859,346 x 300 x 2) in 170 ms, i.e. about 0.8 TFLOP/s effective on Accelerate. A Metal fp16/fp32 matmul on an M1 Pro (Apple quotes about 5 TFLOP/s fp32 for the 16-core GPU [memory]) could be faster but must decode int8 on the GPU and select top-k there (MLX has `argpartition`/`topk`-style ops [memory]); the unified memory makes zero-copy plausible but I found nothing documenting wrapping an mmap'd npy without a copy. Note `[tool.importlinter]` forbids mlx and torch in fse; this would need a separate optional package. Not recommended now.
docs/research/vector-db-survey.md:139:| 1 | Integer int8 prefilter with provable bound, exact rescoring of survivors (section 3.1) | yes, bit-identical | Large for 1 to 8 queries on int8 stores: could approach memory bandwidth; my estimate 2 to 5x, to be measured. No effect on float stores or on BLAS-regime queries | High | `_native_kernel_search.h`, `_native.pyx`, `_backends/_cpu.py`, `_base.py`, `_reference.py`, `_store/_search.py`, tests/test_conformance.py |
