docs/research/store-writer-thread.md:35:- Kafka Java producer bounds bytes: `buffer.memory` (default 32 MiB) "total bytes of memory the producer can use to buffer records", `send()` blocks up to `max.block.ms` then throws; `batch.size` 16 KiB; `linger.ms` default 5. https://kafka.apache.org/41/configuration/producer-configs/
docs/research/store-writer-thread.md:40:- Bound by bytes (what costs memory), with an item cap as a secondary guard: e.g. `maxsize=16` items AND a byte budget of about 64 to 128 MiB (4 to 8 max-size tasks), enforced by a `threading.Condition` counter or `BoundedSemaphore` acquired in `put` with `timeout`, released by the writer after `pwrite`+hash. 16 x 16 MiB = 256 MiB worst case is the number to reduce. Always allow one oversized item through when the budget is empty, to avoid deadlock. Blocking `put` is the right backpressure (Kafka semantics); consider a timeout that raises, as `max.block.ms` does.
docs/research/store-writer-thread.md:58:- Pros: no queue, no extra thread, no copy, memory bound = number of workers; the convoy disappears if the write happens inside the existing GIL-free section; write parallelism on NVMe.
docs/research/store-writer-thread.md:71:2. Buffer lifetime: the caller must not reuse or free the memory (numpy array, mmap) before the writer is done; keep a reference in the queue item.
