docs/research/store-fast-writes.md:3:Scope: about 20 min of web research. Tags: [V] = read in a primary source this session; [S] = secondary/indirect; [U] = unverified, my inference or from memory, measure before relying. No benchmark of our exact workload was found; the web has little hard data on multi-threaded buffered writes to one file, so the main recommendation rests on mechanism plus our own measurements.
docs/research/store-fast-writes.md:15:- posix_fadvise(DONTNEED) only drops clean pages; must come after the writeback (`sync_file_range` or fsync). [U from memory; man page not fetched]. macOS has no posix_fadvise.
docs/research/store-fast-writes.md:56:- x86 with SHA-NI: roughly 1.5 to 2 GB/s per core on Intel Ice Lake/Zen [U, from memory; no source fetched]; without SHA-NI about 0.4 to 0.6 GB/s.
docs/research/store-fast-writes.md:79:3. Hasher thread per tensor: consumes completed task buffers strictly in order (reorder buffer), `hashlib.sha256().update(memoryview)` (GIL released for > 2048 bytes). Buffers are returned to a pool after both pwrite and hash are done, so memory is bounded.
docs/research/store-fast-writes.md:82:Expected gain [U, estimates]: pwrite from workers instead of the calling thread removes the serial copy, aiming at 2.4 to 2.8 M rows/s; replacing zero-fill by ftruncate recovers about 15 %; moving sha256 to a pipeline thread recovers another 15 % if a spare core exists (10 cores, so yes); a background F_FULLFSYNC recovers most of the last 15 to 20 % when the disk keeps up. The in-memory bound of 3.1 M is probably not reached because the page-cache copy still costs memory bandwidth (about 1 to 2 GB/s extra traffic, small vs M1 Pro ~200 GB/s) and the kernel's dirty-page throttling; expect 80 to 90 % of it.
docs/research/store-sinks.md:8:- Header: .npy v1.0 header is padded with spaces and ends in "\n" so magic+length+header is a multiple of 64 bytes (ARRAY_ALIGN = 64); v1.0 header max 65535 bytes, v2.0 up to 4 GiB. [V] https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.lib.format.html and https://github.com/numpy/numpy/blob/main/numpy/lib/_format_impl.py (comment: alignment "supports memory mapping ... where mmap() offset must be page-aligned"). The header is fixed at creation, so shape (row count) must be final when the file is created. Data offset is 64-aligned, not page-aligned; np.memmap handles this by mapping from the page below and offsetting.
docs/research/store-sinks.md:14:- Windows: FlushViewOfFile starts writeback of dirty pages but does not flush metadata nor wait for the hardware cache; call FlushFileBuffers afterwards. A mapping larger than the file extends the file at CreateFileMapping; mapping objects have fixed size; a zero-length file cannot be mapped (ERROR_FILE_INVALID). [V] https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/memoryapi/nf-memoryapi-flushviewoffile, https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/memory/creating-a-file-mapping-object
docs/research/store-sinks.md:65:- USearch: `exact=True` brute-force search; i8 quantization "only valid for cosine-like metrics"; memory-mapped `view`. [V] https://github.com/unum-cloud/usearch. Its claim "up to 20x faster than IndexFlatL2" is a vendor Colab number [unverified independently].
docs/research/store-sinks.md:79:- autofaiss: reads a directory of .npy in lexicographical order; only IVF indexes can be memory-mapped by FAISS (`IO_FLAG_MMAP`). [V] https://github.com/criteo/autofaiss
docs/research/store-sinks.md:81:- sentence-transformers: encode returns an in-memory array; I could not confirm any disk streaming/sharded output in its docs (page truncated). [U] https://sbert.net/docs/package_reference/sentence_transformer/SentenceTransformer.html
docs/architecture/hub.md:5:`Encoder.from_pretrained(repo_or_path, *, revision=None, token=None, cache_dir=None, local_files_only=False, truncate=True, max_length="model", truncate_dim="model", normalize="model", mmap=False)` gives the encoder of a pretrained model. It converts a model of another library on every call, in memory (nothing is hosted, the license of the model applies unchanged), or loads an fse encoder.
docs/architecture/hub.md:16:- **One read per file.** Every file of a model is read once, through one handle of a regular file (never a link, FIFO or other kind), within its size limit (`config.json` and `modules.json` 1 MiB, `README.md` 16 MiB, `tokenizer.json` 64 MiB), into immutable bytes, and a file that changes while it is read raises. Those bytes are what is checked against the Hub commit and what is parsed, so a blob changed after its check changes nothing. An fse encoder of the Hub is therefore read into memory; `mmap` is for local files and raises for a Hub repository (save the encoder and load the file with `mmap`).
docs/architecture/numerical-contract.md:9:- Dimensions are limited to `MAX_DIM` (2**30), because the loops index a row with `int`. Batches are read-only views; the kernel checks their offsets again before it reads through raw pointers, and `out` must not overlap the rows, weights or the batch. `pool_text` (`_native_task.pxi`), which pools a batch straight from the buffers of a `Workspace`, makes the same checks in C on those buffers (the rules and messages of `Batch`, the sentence count of the chunk, then those of `_check_batch`), and the overlap of `out` with the kernel's arrays by their byte ranges, which is the bounds test of `np.may_share_memory` for C-contiguous arrays; a batch in a workspace's own buffers cannot overlap `out`.
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:16:Tags: **[verified]** = I read it in the cited file/page this session. **[derived]** = my own reasoning or arithmetic, not a published result. **[toy]** = my own tiny synthetic experiment (see 5.4), not evidence about real embeddings. **[memory]** = background knowledge not re-checked. **[unverified]** = secondhand or not checked. Permalink form: `https://github.com/<repo>/blob/<commit>/<path>#L<a>-L<b>`; below I give `path:lines` and the commit from the table.
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:22:3. **Two-stage "small in-memory sidecar, exact rescoring from disk" exists in both** (DiskANN: PQ codes in RAM, `FULL_PRECISION_REORDER_MULTIPLIER 3` x k full vectors re-read from disk; knowhere: `refine`/`refine_k` = `k_factor`, and the `DATA_VIEW` refiner that keeps only codes and reads raw data from the segment). **In both, the candidate count is a heuristic multiplier, so the result is approximate.** DiskANN's own range-query correction is statistical (mean +- k sigma of the distance ratio, RFC 00106), not a bound. No library publishes a worst-case bound that makes the two-stage result provably identical. [verified]
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:40:- Search (`src/fse/_store/_search.py:76-120`, `_tasks` at 247-253): row blocks of 4096 to 65 536 rows (`_TASK_SCORES = 1 << 20` scores per task), query slices of 256; for each row block the tasks of all query slices are consecutive, run by at most `workers` threads with a window of `2 * workers` tasks (`_run`, 256-272); every task starts with the **floors merged so far** (`candidates.floor[qb]`, line 227-228). Blocks are slices of memory-mapped npy files (`_layout.py:216`, `NpyContainer().open(..., mmap=True)`); nothing prefetches, advises or aligns, so each worker faults its own pages in.
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:48:- **FLAT index** (`src/index/flat/flat.cc:75-157`, knowhere): `Search` pushes **one task per query** to the global search thread pool (`search_pool_->push`, line 100-101); each task sets OpenMP to 1 thread (`ScopedSearchOmpSetter setter(1)`, line 104), calls `checkCancellation(op_context)` first (line 102), then `index_->search(1, query, k, ...)` with `nq = 1` (line 122). Consequence [derived]: each query scans the whole index on its own, so memory/disk traffic is multiplied by the number of queries; there is no sharing of the pass. (fse reads each row block once for up to 256 queries.)
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:64:- **`DATA_VIEW` refiner** (`src/index/data_view_dense_index/data_view_index_config.h:20-31, 118-127`; `index_node_with_data_view_refiner.h:478-526`): the index keeps **only the quantized codes**, "not alloc extra memory in refiner"; the raw vectors stay in the segment (mmap/columns) and `refine_offset_index_->SearchWithIds(...)` rescored `k * refine_ratio` candidate ids against it. Options for an in-memory refiner copy: `FLOAT16_QUANT`, `BFLOAT16_QUANT`, `UINT8_QUANT`; `refine_with_quant` trades a little precision for speed. This is exactly the "small sidecar in RAM, exact rescoring from the stored rows" architecture. Milvus sets it for its **growing-segment interim index**: `queryNode.segcore.interimIndex` (`milvus.yaml:673-686`): `denseVectorIndexType: IVF_FLAT_CC`, `nlist 128`, `nprobe 16`, `refineRatio 4.5` (the compiled default is 3.0, `SegcoreConfig.h:357`), `refineQuantType NONE`, `refineWithQuant true`, `indexBuildRatio 0.1`. `IndexConfigGenerator.cpp:97-105` puts `nprobe`, `refine_ratio`, `refine_with_quant` into the search parameters.
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:70:- **Growing segment** (`internal/core/src/segcore/SegmentGrowingImpl.*`): in-memory columns split into chunks of `chunkRows: 128` rows (`milvus.yaml:672`); searched by brute force or by the interim index above (built when `indexBuildRatio` of the max rows is reached); `queryNode.mmap.growingMmapEnabled: false` (`milvus.yaml:759`) can back growing raw data with mmap.
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:72:- **Flush** (`internal/flushcommon/syncmgr/task.go:128-218`, `meta_writer.go:101-160`): a `SyncTask` writes the binlog files (insert, delta, stats, bm25) to object storage with retries, **then** calls `SaveBinlogPaths` (data coord) with the segment's binlog paths and `StartPositions` (the WAL checkpoint) and `Flushed`, **then** updates the in-memory cache. Order: data first, pointer second, which is the same discipline as fse's "files, manifest, rename". Data node buffers: `insertBufSize` 16 MiB, `deleteBufBytes` 16 MiB, `syncPeriod` 600 s, memory-pressure force sync at `forceSyncWatermark 0.5` (`milvus.yaml:1086-1100`).
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:80:- Configuration (`milvus.yaml:750-761`): `queryNode.mmap.vectorField: true`, `vectorIndex: false`, scalar fields/indexes false, `growingMmapEnabled: false`. Docs (https://milvus.io/docs/mmap.md, fetched): "when the amount of data exceeds memory by too much, search and query performance may suffer serious degradation"; HNSW recommended for mmap, IVF-series "dropped seriously" in Milvus's tests; **mmap not supported for DiskANN** (which does its own `O_DIRECT` I/O); NVMe recommended; the page does not mention the OS page cache.
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:81:- Mechanism (`internal/core/src/common/ChunkTarget.cpp:62-100`): a chunk is written to a local file through `FileWriter`, padded to capacity, then `mmap(PROT_READ, MAP_SHARED | (populate ? MAP_POPULATE : 0))`. Warm-up: `queryNode.segcore.tieredStorage.warmup.vectorField: disable` by default (lazy page faults), `vectorIndex: sync` (`milvus.yaml:693-706`). Tiered storage has its own cache with watermarks `memory/diskLow 0.75`, `High 0.8`, `evictionEnabled: false` by default, time-based `cacheTtl` (`milvus.yaml:708-735`).
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:105:- **Overlap in the original is across queries, not within one**: each search thread has its own context and blocks on its own reads; concurrency of many query threads fills the SSD queue. PipeANN (OSDI 2025, Guo and Lu) replaces the synchronous beam with an asynchronous pipeline that overlaps CPU and I/O; reported by the authors: latency 1.14x to 2.02x of in-memory Vamana and ~35 % of DiskANN's on billion-scale data (abstract as summarized by papers.cool via search; **[unverified]**, not read in full; others report GIST-like high dimensions become compute-bound).
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:108:- **Flat scan exists in the Rust DiskANN** (`SearchMode::FlatScan`, `search_mode.rs:22-72`; `flat_search`, `disk_provider.rs:1157-1210`): a brute-force pass over the **in-memory quantized data**, top `neighbors_before_reranking`, then the same `RerankAndFilter` against full-precision vectors read from the disk index. Doc comment: "The top `neighbors_before_reranking` candidates from the quantized scan will be provided to full-precision reranking." This is the closest existing implementation of the two-stage scan; it is a **recall baseline**, not exact, and candidate count is a user parameter. The design RFC (`rfcs/00983-flat-search.md`, 2026-04-24) states the scan is sequential, single-threaded per query (`DistancesUnordered` takes `&mut self`, "precluding internal parallelism ... left for future work"), and lists batched distance computation "for better cache utilization" as an open alternative. Post-processing/rerank for flat search was still "planned" in the RFC.
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:114:- Fresh-DiskANN (arXiv 2105.09613; PDF text extracted locally): a long-term SSD index (LTI) plus in-memory TempIndexes (one read-write, several read-only snapshots) and a DeleteList; updates go to a **redo log** and TempIndex snapshots; **StreamingMerge** periodically folds them into a new LTI. Section 5.4: "The procedure does exactly two sequential passes over the SSD-resident data structure in the Delete and Patch Phases", plus about 100 random 4 KB reads per inserted point in the insert phase; memory ~`O(|N| R)` (~7 GB for 30 M points, R = 64). Evaluation: "StreamingMerge with 40 threads takes around 16000 seconds to merge 30M inserts and deletes into a 800M point LTI (a 7.5% change), which is 8.5% of the ~190000 seconds it would take to rebuild the index from scratch"; 3600 updates/s need 40 threads; with 10 threads, 1000 updates/s with more predictable search latency. Abstract: 5-10x lower cost of maintaining freshness than periodic rebuilds.
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:115:- Rust README (`README.md:16`): real-time updates "using logic from IP-DiskANN and Fresh-DiskANN ... no merges, rebuilds, patches needed"; dynamic in-memory index is separate (`diskann-inmem`), and the disk index is still built offline.
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:129:| `madvise` / `posix_fadvise` man pages (fetched) | `POSIX_FADV_SEQUENTIAL` "doubles" the read-ahead size; `POSIX_FADV_WILLNEED` "initiates a nonblocking read of the specified region into the page cache" (may load less under memory pressure); `POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED` frees clean cached pages, dirty pages must be synced first, only whole pages; `MADV_COLD`/`MADV_PAGEOUT` (since Linux 5.4), `MADV_POPULATE_READ` (5.14) | the building blocks for a prefetcher and for scan resistance |
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:143:The pattern both projects use: stage 1 cheaply scores everything from a small in-memory structure; stage 2 re-reads the **full stored rows** of the survivors and rescoring uses the existing kernel, so the final scores are bit-identical to the contract. Whether the final top-k is also identical depends only on whether stage 1 can lose a true top-k row.
docs/research/vector-db-milvus-diskann.md:181:3. **`fdatasync` instead of `fsync` on Linux** for the vector file (Milvus uses `fdatasync` on non-Apple, `FileWriter.cpp:215-220`): size changes from `shrink` are metadata `fdatasync` must flush anyway [memory of the man page], so the saving is only the mtime/ctime journal commit; negligible against 256 MiB.
docs/architecture/persistence.md:13:- A **checksum** is `xxh3-128:` and the 32 lowercase hex digits of XXH3-128 with seed 0 (in the canonical big-endian form, as `xxhash.xxh3_128().hexdigest()` gives it) of bytes fed in order; a tensor's checksum is that of its little-endian C-order bytes, and a tensor without elements has the checksum of no bytes, `xxh3-128:99aa06d3014798d86001c324468d497f`. `fse._container._hash` is the only module that computes one and the only one that imports a hash library (`hash-importers` of the guardrails); it streams (`update` releases the GIL for large inputs), takes bytes, C-contiguous memory and C-contiguous little-endian arrays only, and the containers feed it in chunks. Values are compared as whole strings, the algorithm included.
docs/architecture/text.md:5:`fse._text` turns text into batches (stages 1 and 2). A source holds its sentences as one UTF-8 buffer: strings in memory with their offsets, or the memory-mapped lines of a file. `encode` takes texts in memory as a list, tuple or one-dimensional numpy array either of str or of lists or tuples of str (`fse._encoder._inputs`): the first row decides which, a row of the other kind raises with both rows named, and the tokenizer is checked before any row; a path and a stream are passed to it directly. (`fse.Corpus`, which held such a source for the passes of fit, was removed with fit: converting a list of str costs about 3 ms for 400,000 sentences since the native join.) Strings and token lists (the external tokenizer) are joined into that buffer in one native pass, a string as its UTF-8 (`join_strings`) and the tokens of a sentence separated by one space (`join_token_lists`): it reads exact lists, tuples and str through borrowed pointers, checks every str (no lone surrogate; a token also non-empty and without a code point of W) and writes its UTF-8 at once, in parts of at least 4,096 sentences that up to 8 native threads run while the calling thread holds the GIL and waits. No Python code can run during the pass (no calls, no references dropped, no Python object allocated, and no other Python thread gets the GIL; this is why the extension refuses to compile for free-threaded Python, `Py_GIL_DISABLED`), and every str object, scattered in memory, is read once: that read is what the pass costs. 85.7 million WikiText tokens take 0.65 s on an M1 Pro (2.1 s in one thread, 11 s in Python), and 400,000 strings of 20 tokens 3 ms instead of the 70 ms of `str.encode` per string, so `Encoder.encode` of such a list takes 0.64 of its former time on the cpu at 8 workers (every StringSource, so a list or tuple of str passed to `encode`; a `StreamSource` still encodes each string in the calling thread); a second pass to size the buffer first doubled the time, prefetching the next tokens gained nothing measurable; a buffer grows into a new block with a copy, not with `realloc`, because on macOS the blocks of a freed chain of large reallocs stayed resident and the RSS grew by about the joined bytes on every call. Other types (subclasses, which may override `encode` or `split`, a numpy array, a legacy string of 3.11) and every invalid str go through the Python path, which raises the errors in their order: for strings the type and then the UTF-8 of each sentence in turn, for token lists the types and tokens of all sentences first, then the UTF-8 of every sentence. A buffer of a successful native join is valid UTF-8 by construction (it reads only exact, ready str, copies ASCII, writes every other code point in its shortest form, fails on a surrogate, and CPython keeps code points at most U+10FFFF; it writes the offsets itself, from 0 to the length of the data), so it is marked verified, its offsets are made read-only, and it is not scanned again: that saves 1.8 ms of the 10 ms of WikiText at 10 workers before the first task (M1 Pro). Every other buffer (the Python path, `StringSource.encoded` and so every collection of a stream, a file, an Arrow column) is scanned; a broken proof would still be caught, because every tokenizer reports invalid UTF-8 (also behind a cut) and `TextLookup.encode` checks the offsets of every range. `scan` validates the UTF-8 of every other sentence in threads without the GIL and cuts the source into chunks of about 64 KiB to 1 MiB (at most `MAX_CHUNK_ROWS` sentences): strings and token lists from their offsets (`fse._text._plan.offset_cuts`: k = max(ceil(bytes / chunk_bytes), ceil(sentences / chunk_rows)) pieces that start at the first sentence at or after `j * bytes // k`, so where the rows bind the pieces follow them instead of each being split again (that made a stream of short sentences 51 tasks instead of 39 and 7 % slower at 10 workers), and a piece of more rows still split into parts of about equal rows, so a chunk holds at most `chunk_bytes` plus its last sentence, and the targets inside one sentence are skipped, so the work is one step per cut), the lines of a file and Arrow rows by the scan itself (a chunk ends after the sentence that reaches `chunk_bytes`, and the rest of a scan range joins the next one); `encode` turns one chunk into a Batch with a `TextLookup` (the tokenizer, the `VocabTable` built from the vocabulary bytes of the word vectors, and the FastText buckets) and a `Workspace`, the buffers one worker reuses. `make_vocabulary` builds the `VocabTable` of word vectors once as a `Vocabulary`; every lookup of the same words in the same order (checked by `WordVectors.vocabulary_fingerprint`, the fingerprint of `vocab.bytes` and `vocab.offsets` alone) can share it, so an encoder builds it once for all its backends and passes. `fse._text_file.FileSource` reads the lines of a file. Every sentence is one row, empty ones included. Tokens are matched to the vocabulary by their exact UTF-8 bytes, so with `fse_words@1` a word with uppercase letters or whitespace (such as `New_York` in GoogleNews) is never matched. `tools/make_unicode_tables.py` generates the lowercase tables from `UnicodeData.txt`; both generated files are checked in and compared with each other in the tests. With `wordpiece@1` the encoder's `TokenizerSpec` (`fse._tokenizer_spec`: the identifier, its parameters, `fse.unknown` and the length rule, immutable) goes through `EncodeSpec` and the `Pipeline` to `make_lookup`, which builds a `WordPieceState` once from it and the vocabulary: the `VocabTable` of all tokens, a byte trie of all tokens (`_native_wordpiece_trie.h`, a static double array built at load from the vocabulary bytes in about 14 ms and 0.7 MiB for potion-base-8M, 32 ms and 1.6 MiB for potion-base-32M; never persisted) with the node of the continuation prefix, and the added tokens; a sentence is then split at the added tokens, normalized and pre-tokenized into a buffer of the `Workspace` and matched word by word, without the GIL. The native path has fast paths that give the ids of the plain rules: the scan for added tokens jumps to the bytes they start with (`memchr` for one such byte); ASCII bytes are normalized through 128-byte tables derived from the composed Unicode table when the module loads (8 printable bytes lowercased at once), but only while no combining marks wait in a run (an ASCII byte ends a run, a removed one does not), and pre-tokenized by a class table (8 bytes of a-z and 0-9 skipped at once); the length rule counts 8 ASCII bytes at a time and still validates the whole sentence; the code points of a word are counted only when it has more bytes than the word limit; a word is first one lookup of the `VocabTable`, and after a miss each piece is the longest token the trie reads from the root or the prefix node at a code-point boundary, which is what the greedy loop of lookups returns. The module refuses to build a state when the Unicode table gives an ASCII byte a shape the tables cannot hold. A private switch (`_generic`, for the tests, never persisted) takes the plain rules for every step, and the tests compare both paths; on an M1 Pro the fast paths make the tokenizer alone 2.16 (WikiText-103) and 1.71 (MASSIVE) times as fast at one thread, and `Encoder.encode` of potion-base-8M 1.64 and 1.37 times at one worker.
docs/architecture/text.md:7:**Arrow string columns.** `fse._text._arrow.ArrowSource` reads a column that any producer exports through the Arrow PyCapsule interface (`__arrow_c_stream__`, else `__arrow_c_array__`; https://arrow.apache.org/docs/format/CDataInterface/PyCapsuleInterface.html), so fse needs no pyarrow: pyarrow arrays and chunked arrays, pandas 3 Series (`large_string`), Polars Series (`string_view`, whatever schema is requested) and Hugging Face datasets in Arrow format (`string`, chunks of 1,000 rows, memory-mapped). `encode` and the heads check for the protocol before the collection rules, every batch of `encode_batches` may be such a column, and its rows come from the source, not from `len()`; a producer that cannot export (pandas without pyarrow) is read as a stream with a warning, and a `datasets` Column, which exports nothing, raises with the Arrow-format path. No schema is requested. The native part (`_native_arrow.pxi`, `_native_arrow_column.pxi`, `_native_arrow.h`) moves the arrays out of their capsules in the calling thread, with the GIL, and owns them until `ArrowSource.close` at the end of the call, when each is released once, with the GIL (never while a scan or encode reads it); the stream is read whole when the source is made, so its rows are known and the scan checks every row before the first is encoded. It reads `utf8` (`u`), `large_utf8` (`U`) and `utf8_view` (`vu`) where they are: a row is `(pointer, length)` from its int32 or int64 offsets or from its 16-byte view (inline up to 12 bytes, else a variadic buffer, index and offset). Rows are numbered across the chunks, and a chunk of the runtime may span many Arrow chunks, so the runtime plans an Arrow source like a `StringSource`, and its batches and rows are those of the same texts as a list, bit for bit (the conformance suites take Arrow columns as input classes). It refuses tables (naming their string columns), binary, dictionary-encoded and extension columns, and ends the column at its first null (reading the validity bitmap whatever the null count says): the rows before it are the column, nothing after it is read, and its error, which names its row, is raised after the rows before it, like an invalid sentence of the scan. Before it forms a pointer from a value it checks: the formats, children, dictionaries and capsule names; lengths, array offsets and every sum and product of them against `INT64_MAX`, `SIZE_MAX` and `PY_SSIZE_T_MAX`; the required buffers; the null count (from -1 to the length; without a bitmap only 0); the first and last offset of a chunk; the sizes of the view buffers (read with `memcpy`); and, per row as it is read, offsets within the data up to the chunk's last offset, and views within their buffer's stated size; then the UTF-8 as for every source. Offsets or views that are not aligned are copied once into an aligned buffer and logged; the text is never copied. **The producer's obligations**, which the C Data Interface leaves to it and no consumer can check: every buffer is at least as large as its layout implies (the bitmap, the offsets or views up to `offset + length`, the data up to the last offset, each view buffer of its stated size), and no buffer changes while fse reads it. Under these obligations fse never reads outside a buffer; a producer that breaks them gives an error or wrong rows that fse cannot detect.
docs/architecture/text.md:9:`fse._stream.StreamSource` takes sentences from any iterable (a queue consumer, a crawler, log lines) and is read once. The runtime reads it in the calling thread, a collection of at most `chunk_rows` sentences (65,536) or about `chunk_bytes` (8 MiB) at a time, and only when its window of tasks has room, so a slow sink slows the source and memory stays bounded by one collection and the window. A sentence that is no str or has no UTF-8 form, and an error of the iterator, are raised after the rows before them were committed, with the row number counted from the start of the stream. The iterator is never read from another thread. One thread reads a stream, so a stream gives at most what that thread can collect: about 2.8 million sentences per second on a 16-vCPU EPYC (`bench_runtime.py stream`), the same as a StringSource up to about 8 workers and 0.8 to 0.9 of it at 16; a native reader may lift this later.
docs/architecture/runtime.md:7:- **Tasks.** A source is scanned into chunks, one task each: about `TASKS_PER_WORKER` (2) per worker, `ceil(bytes / (2 * workers))` bytes each within 64 KiB and 1 MiB (`fse._text._scan.default_chunk_bytes`), and at most 16 MiB of finished rows (`TASK_ROW_BYTES`) and the rows the window leaves a task. A task took the GIL back at about ten points (its native stages and the numpy checks around them): at one worker that cost about 19 µs, with 10 threads that hand the GIL around about 0.2 to 0.34 ms of CPU and more wall time (M1 Pro), so the default rule of 8 tasks per worker made WikiText (2.4 MB) 29 tasks of about 1 ms that waited for the GIL in 46 % of their samples; two tasks per worker leave room to balance cores of different speed. The automatic plan (`chunk_bytes=None`, more than one worker, not inline, not a stream) also splits its tail: the last `workers` chunks are each cut into two parts of about equal rows (`_Buffered.split_tail`, in the calling thread before any task runs, by `prefix` and the rows, so a file needs one newline search per cut), because the workers that finish last would otherwise idle while the last whole chunks run (DR-180). A chunk is cut only if both parts keep the minimum chunk size (64 KiB), so a source of chunks near that size is not split. The cuts are a function of the chunks and the workers alone, the chunks stay contiguous, in row order and non-empty, an explicit `chunk_bytes` keeps its cuts, and the rows, the errors and the GIL budget of a task do not change (only the tasks differ in number and size). On the cpu backend a task now runs its stages 1 to 4 in one section without the GIL (`text_rows`, `_native.pool_text`), with no numpy call that releases it; storage into float16 or int8 adds one section (`store_rows`) and its overlap checks, inverse norms one more. On an M1 Pro the workers then wait for the GIL in 10 % of their samples instead of 36 % (WikiText, 10 workers), and potion-base-8M encodes WikiText 1.25 times and MASSIVE 1.05 times as fast at 10 workers, with the same bits. `tests/test_runtime_gil.py` holds these budgets per task exactly: it counts the sections fse enters (every one on the task path increments `_native._test_nogil_sections` right before it, which a check of the Cython source enforces) and every call of a numpy function, ufunc (and ufunc method) and ndarray method, blocking call and lock in a task, and fails when one more appears. No CPython API counts GIL acquisitions (`PyEval_SaveThread` and `PyEval_RestoreThread` are what `Py_BEGIN_ALLOW_THREADS` expands to, and the GIL's switch counter is private), so ndarray operators and methods in Cython code are invisible to it; the source check forbids them, `with gil` and `except *` in `_native_task.pxi`, and requires `noexcept` of every cdef function its nogil blocks call, directly or through each other (without it Cython checks for an exception after each call, and a void function takes the GIL back for that). `out` overlaps an array only if both hold bytes, as `np.may_share_memory` says. Sources from about 20 MiB at 10 workers get chunks of 1 MiB, as before. A chunk holds at most its bytes plus its last sentence, and a sentence (up to 2**30 bytes) is never split, so a task of one long sentence takes as long as that sentence. Besides its rows a task holds its batch (4 bytes per token id and FastText bucket, at most `max_ngrams` buckets per unknown word, 8 per sentence and unknown word for the offsets) and the workspace of its thread (about `2 n + 32` bytes for the candidates of its longest sentence of n bytes and 16 bytes per token of it), which the thread reuses; at most the window's tasks hold them at once. A stream is read in collections of up to 65,536 sentences, each scanned into 8 tasks (`STREAM_TASKS_PER_COLLECTION`: a collection is not the whole input) with rows counted from the start of the stream.
docs/architecture/runtime.md:10:- **Window.** At most `2 * workers` tasks are in flight or waiting to be committed, and their rows take at most 512 MiB (`WINDOW_BYTES`; with many workers a task holds fewer rows; every row counts its target, the float32 rows before a float16 or int8 target is stored, and with `normalize` 12 bytes per value (`STEP_ROW_BYTES`): normalize runs in place, but without this term its tasks were up to twice as large and the peak RSS of a stream at 10 workers grew by 65 to 140 MiB for at most 10 % throughput on an M1 Pro, because the rest of a task grows with its rows too, so the term stays): a slow sink slows the source, and memory stays bounded by the window and one collection of a stream.
docs/architecture/runtime.md:12:- **Sinks.** A sink (`fse.Sink`) takes the rows of a run: `target` and `commit` in the calling thread, in row order, each row once; a target is valid only until its commit. Optional parts: `dtype`, `inverse_norms` (the target ends with a float32 vector the worker fills with the inverse norm of every stored row, so a store computes none in the calling thread; the window counts it), `encoder_fingerprint` (the fingerprint of the only encoder it takes), `flush()` after the last commit of a successful run, `publish()`, which `encode_batches` calls after the rows of every batch are committed and before it reads `durable_rows`, and `durable_rows`, the leading rows of the run that are durable. A sink takes one run; a second run into it starts again at row 0, which it refuses. `Pipeline.infer` numbers the rows of a sink from `first_row`, so the batches of a stream continue the rows of a run. `Encoder.encode(texts, sink=)` commits to a sink and returns nothing; `encode_batches(batches)` encodes a stream batch by batch, each a finite list (as a queue consumer polls them), and gives one `EncodedBatch` per batch once its rows are finished: its rows without a sink, else the sink's `durable_rows`, so a consumer confirms its input only up to there (at least once: after a crash it reads again from the last confirmed offset). That count is a snapshot after the sink's `publish`: the sink's `flush` runs after the last batch, so after a successful full iteration the sink's own `durable_rows` says what is durable (for a full-durability ShardSink every row), and the last batch may report fewer. Stopping early does not flush; the owner of the sink closes it. With `ShardSink(durability="none")`, batch and sink `durable_rows` stay zero and cannot acknowledge durable input. The sink's `published_rows` still tracks visible rows. `MemorySink` keeps the rows of a run in memory and has no `durable_rows`.
docs/research/vector-db-storage-durability.md:28:- Docs [doc, duckdb.org/docs/2.0/core_extensions/vss.html]: persistence only with the experimental flag; "there are no incremental updates to persistent index storage"; "every time DuckDB performs a checkpoint the entire index will be serialized to disk and overwrite itself"; "WAL recovery is not yet properly implemented for custom indexes", so an unexpected shutdown can mean "data loss or corruption of the index"; the index "is not buffer managed and must be able to fit into RAM" and does not count toward `memory_limit`. The README says the same, plus that the index is reloaded in full on first access after restart.
docs/research/vector-db-storage-durability.md:43:- Prune with bit-identical compare: `rust/lance-index/src/vector/bq/prune.rs` header: after a binary scan, each row's lower bound is checked against the query upper bound and the current heap threshold; "only the survivors (typically well under 1%) are reranked" (author comment, not a measured table); the kernels use "exactly the operation order of the scalar helper, multiplies and adds, never FMA" and ordered-quiet `>=` so "the masks reproduce the scalar `>=` decisions exactly"; a stale heap threshold "can only over-select survivors, never wrongly prune" [src]. This is the closest published design to the exact int8 prefilter (derived in `vector-db-tricks.md` 3.1) and supports its two rules: keep the survivor test monotone (stale thresholds only add survivors) and keep compare semantics identical to the scalar reference. Exact for the reranked set only if the bound is valid; Lance uses it on an approximate (IVF_RQ) index, so its overall result is approximate.
docs/research/vector-db-storage-durability.md:47:- `Scanner::refine(factor)` (`scanner.rs` ~2668): "reading extra elements and using the original vector values to re-rank the distances ... if factor is 2, then the search will read 2x more elements than the requested k before performing the re-ranking. Even if the factor is 1, the results will still be re-ranked" [src]. The ANN sub-index is asked for `k * refine_factor` (`knn.rs` ~1685, 3086) [src]. LanceDB docs: `refine_factor` "Reads and reranks additional candidates in memory to recover recall lost to quantization"; `bypass_vector_index()` forces "an exact (flat) scan"; "exact queries may be prohibitively slow on production scales" [doc, docs.lancedb.com/indexing/vector-index]. Approximate (user-chosen multiplier), same as Qdrant/Weaviate.
docs/research/vector-db-storage-durability.md:59:- MemWAL (Lance's streaming WAL, `rust/lance/src/dataset/mem_wal.rs` ~27): "Durable writes: wait for WAL flush before returning; Non-durable writes: buffer in memory, accept potential loss on crash" [src]. A per-write durability flag, comparable to RocksDB `WriteOptions::sync`.
docs/research/vector-db-storage-durability.md:129:- "Are you sure you want to use MMAP in your DBMS?" (CIDR 2022, db.cs.cmu.edu/mmap-cidr2022/): the page summary lists no numbers; the paper and `github.com/viktorleis/mmapbench` hold the measurements; not read here. Relevant caveat for fse: page-cache eviction and TLB cost under memory pressure are the paper's main mmap complaints [paper abstract, partial].
